WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.019 --> 00:00:02.436
(soft music)

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.033
- Oh, I feel like I slept
on a pile of bricks.

00:00:10.291 --> 00:00:11.520
- Well, I slept like a baby,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.260
knowing that all our cash is safe

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.780
and sound right in our mattress.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.040
- No wonder my back hurts.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.000
Why'd you do that?

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.950
- Well with all these banks closing,

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.750
I just figured it was the safest place

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.220
to put our money right now.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.020
- You know the banks are insured, right?

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.460
- Well, yeah, but what if the insurance

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.080
companies go under too?

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.460
- I think that's a lot less likely

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.040
than someone coming into
our house and stealing it.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.720
- How would anyone ever find out

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.853
we keep all our savings in our mattress?

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.497
Maybe I'll put it back in the bank.

00:00:42.497 --> 00:00:45.080
(upbeat music)

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:51.840
- Most people know that the image

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.090
of all your hard-earned paper dollars

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.570
sitting in some dusty vault
isn't actually how it works.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.570
Though the first banks
were likely ancient temples

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.490
that guarded wealthy people's currency,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.340
the Renaissance brought a
major financial innovation

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.890
called Fractional Reserve Banking,

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.690
which allowed banks to lend out

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.360
the majority of their deposits
at interest of course,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.240
and only keep a fraction of them on hand.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.880
- See, this is exactly
why I'm so freaked out.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:18.832
What happens if everyone wants

00:01:18.832 --> 00:01:22.140
to withdraw their money
at once a la Mary Poppins?

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.370
We'd be out in the cold.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:24.930
I want my tuppence with me.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:25.800
Thank you very much.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.850
- Well, it's true.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.820
Bank runs and failures
were a common consequence

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.130
of fractional reserve banking in the past.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.360
In the mid 1800s,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.750
rampant railroad speculation
led to big losses for banks

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.960
and hundreds failed in
an awful domino effect.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.237
After the Great Depression of 1929,

00:01:42.237 --> 00:01:44.160
FDR stepped in to stabilize things

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.530
with the Banking Act of 1933

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.190
which created the Federal
Deposit Insurance Corporation.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.590
The FDIC is a government corporation

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.810
that provides insurance for depositors.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.960
If a banking institution
covered by the FDIC fails,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.950
account owners are made
whole up to $250,000.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.500
- Now, this insurance is
something you'll never

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.290
have to pay for, at least not directly.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.830
The FDIC is not publicly funded.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.210
They get their money by charging the banks

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:15.660
for their membership based
on the bank's level of risk.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.200
This ranges from well-capitalized,
which means a 10%

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.710
or higher reserves to
debt ratio all the way

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:27.330
to less than 2% known as
critically undercapitalized.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:30.060
The lower the reserves,
the higher the banks dues.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.030
- The FDIC also plays an oversight role.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:37.260
When an insured bank drops to
around the 6% reserves mark,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.960
it can force the bank to
take corrective action

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.140
like change management or sell off assets.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.150
It can also take over failed banks

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.120
and manage the sale of
remaining assets and debts

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.960
like it did for Silicon
Valley Bank in March of 2023.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.090
In that particular case, since so many

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.240
of the depositors had more than $250,000,

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.420
the government made an exception
and reimbursed everyone

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.190
in order to shore up confidence

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:05.580
in the system and prevent
a contagion of collapse.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.319
- So it sounds like I can take a breath.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:12.420
It also makes me feel better
knowing that since 1933,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.570
not a single depositor has lost a penny

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.730
of an FDIC insured deposit

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.010
and the government seems more committed

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.490
than ever in making sure the
banking system is stable.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.770
So I think our checking

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:29.430
and savings accounts are as
safe as I could hope for.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.610
But what about our retirement
funds, our investments?

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.370
And now that I think about
it, we're not even at a bank.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.690
We're with a credit union.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:37.860
- Don't worry, Henny Penny.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.865
There's another acronym
that's got us covered.

00:03:40.865 --> 00:03:43.290
The NCUSIF is basically the same thing,

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.580
but for credit unions.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.080
And as for our IRAs

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.760
and investment accounts, well that's where

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.880
the Securities Investor
Protection Corporation comes in.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.360
The SIPC is actually a
non-profit governmental

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.150
organization that insures
the investment accounts

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:01.410
and member institutions, again,
up to $250,000 per member.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.150
- But keep in mind, these forms

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.960
of insurance only apply if
the institution goes under.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.490
It doesn't mean we're protected

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.130
against our investments losing value.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:15.000
Commodities, fixed annuities,
hedge funds, crypto,

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.590
money you park in these kinds

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.960
of financial instruments is uninsured.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:20.910
That's a big reason why the crypto crash

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.530
was so hard on people.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:23.880
- Overall, the government

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.980
and financial institutions have done a lot

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.560
of work making the
banking system pretty safe

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.050
for your average person,
which means the 48%

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.680
of Americans who report feeling
moderately to very concerned

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.410
about the safety of their
money can relax a bit,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.100
even though times seem
especially turbulent post COVID.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.350
- But in reality, there's no such thing

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.570
as a perfect store of value.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:49.080
Whether it's stuffed in a
savings account or a mattress,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.990
cash is always susceptible to inflation.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.310
Certificates of deposit

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.620
and treasury bills might have really long

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.540
and steady track records, but they're not

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.840
gonna return enough to
help you retire on time.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.840
And as we've talked about
in other episodes, stocks,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.900
mutual funds and even
real estate naturally gain

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.260
and lose value in cycles over time.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.300
There's nothing out there that's perfectly

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.070
impervious to loss.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.440
- But when you find yourself
getting especially nervous,

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.540
try asking yourself the question,

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.720
what are you wanting to
keep your money safe from?

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.820
- If you're nervous about inflation eating

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.250
away the purchasing power
of the cash you have,

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.800
then you could move
some into an investment.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.770
If it's about your company's
stocks losing value,

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.000
you might wanna mitigate that

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.450
by moving it into a broadly
invested mutual fund.

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.640
- But if you're worried
that you might wake up

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.010
and see the name of your bank on the news,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:44.040
making sure your accounts
are with insured institutions

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.000
and under $250,000 per
bank should do the trick.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.220
- [Both Presenters] And
that's our Two Cents

